1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,880
Gastroptosis — wondered if it was just you feeling stuffed after only a few bites?

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:08,160
If you have a naturally thin frame, here's why.

3
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:18,320
A leaner body means less fat and muscle cushioning around your stomach, so the ligaments holding it in place get less support, and the stomach sags lower than normal.

4
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,440
Three everyday habits make that sag worse.

5
00:00:21,760 --> 00:00:24,400
First, eating large meals too fast.

6
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:35,280
When food rushes into a stomach that's already sitting lower than it should, the stomach wall stretches suddenly and digestion can't keep pace, so bloating and pressure hit hard.

7
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,200
Second, slouching or lying down right after eating.

8
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:49,840
Slumped posture and lying flat both add extra pressure on your abdomen, pushing the sagging stomach down even further and making that heavy, pressured feeling linger.

9
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:52,320
Third, weak core muscles.

10
00:00:52,640 --> 00:01:01,120
Your abdominal wall is part of what holds your stomach in place, and when it's weak, the stomach has less support and keeps drifting lower over time.

11
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:08,400
So eat smaller portions spread across four or five meals instead of two or three big ones, and chew slowly.

12
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:22,640
Stay upright for at least 30 minutes after eating instead of slouching or lying down, and if you do lie down, rest on your right side — that helps food move toward your pylorus with gravity instead of sitting heavy in a sagging stomach.

13
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:38,160
Work in exercises that build real abdominal strength, like planks or gentle sit-ups, so your stomach gets more support over time — a gentle seated spinal twist is worth adding too, as an easy way to stay loose without putting extra pressure on your stomach.
